</figure>

With these definitions, we see another aspect of magnitudes, namely, two magnitudes of the same kind, such as two angles, can be compared for size. Euclid frequently uses what is known as the *law of trichotomy*: given two magnitudes *F* and *G* of the same kind, exactly one of the following three situations hold, *F* is less than *G, F* equals *G,* or *F* is greater than *G.* See the comments after the [Common Notions](../../commonnotions/cn1/) for more discussion on magnitudes and the law of trichotomy.
